WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.950
Welcome to the Federal
Modernization Workbook's

2
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:05.638
developer workbook,
Building Azure Security.

3
00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:08.725
This is Chapter 1: the
landing-zone baseline.

4
00:00:08.725 --> 00:00:13.102
A baseline nobody reads back is a
wish, so the first thing the kit does

5
00:00:13.102 --> 00:00:17.400
is read it back, the same way, every
week, with nothing it could change.

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.013
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's own
commands, see what they leave to you,

7
00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:24.704
read the kit's spine
and its baseline check,

8
00:00:24.704 --> 00:00:27.625
and walk two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:30.640
The chapter is a draft
awaiting the owner's review,

10
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.910
and the code is illustrative,
tested against a fictional estate.

11
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.710
Why read the baseline back as code?

12
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.581
Because the guide's Step
1 sets four things once,

13
00:00:43.581 --> 00:00:47.335
and nobody checks them every
week across every subscription.

14
00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:49.896
By the end of this chapter
you can read all four:

15
00:00:49.896 --> 00:00:54.884
whether creating management groups needs
authorization and new subscriptions land in

16
00:00:54.884 --> 00:00:59.467
a default group, whether the required
initiatives sit at the intermediate root in

17
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:03.781
audit, whether every platform resource
sends logs to the platform workspace,

18
00:01:03.781 --> 00:01:07.960
and whether every subscription has
the required Defender for Cloud plans.

19
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.672
It's for platform engineers
who own the hierarchy,

20
00:01:10.672 --> 00:01:14.772
and for the developers and security
officers who review their automation.

21
00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:18.323
And one rule holds throughout:
every read is declared,

22
00:01:18.323 --> 00:01:23.840
every finding names its source, and
nothing the kit runs can change the estate.

23
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.465
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

24
00:01:29.465 --> 00:01:33.999
Learn's Az PowerShell for protecting the
hierarchy sets two things on the tenant

25
00:01:33.999 --> 00:01:37.721
root group: that creating a
management group needs authorization,

26
00:01:37.721 --> 00:01:40.090
and which group new subscriptions land in.

27
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.353
Learn's REST reference reads
both back with one call.

28
00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:48.278
The guide assigns the security benchmark
and the FedRAMP initiative in DoNotEnforce,

29
00:01:48.278 --> 00:01:51.014
which evaluates compliance
without enforcing.

30
00:01:51.014 --> 00:01:56.004
And two more REST references give the
reads the kit needs: diagnostic settings,

31
00:01:56.004 --> 00:01:59.611
with the workspace and the
log categories each one sends,

32
00:01:59.611 --> 00:02:03.430
and Defender for Cloud's
pricings, with each plan's tier.

33
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.543
What does Learn leave to you?

34
00:02:08.543 --> 00:02:10.840
Each command is run by hand, once,

35
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.905
so nobody runs it every week
across every subscription.

36
00:02:14.905 --> 00:02:17.518
A Resource Graph query with no scope reads

37
00:02:17.518 --> 00:02:20.355
whatever the signed-in
account happens to see.

38
00:02:20.355 --> 00:02:23.294
Comparing diagnostic
settings by eye misses the

39
00:02:23.294 --> 00:02:26.017
one resource that logs
to the wrong workspace.

40
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:29.305
A session left on another
cloud reads another estate.

41
00:02:29.305 --> 00:02:31.249
And nothing records what was read,

42
00:02:31.249 --> 00:02:34.305
so an assessor can't tell
which run a finding came from.

43
00:02:34.305 --> 00:02:38.510
The kit answers each of those,
and a test pins each answer.

44
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.498
Now the kit's spine.

45
00:02:43.498 --> 00:02:46.323
One call list declares thirty-three reads:

46
00:02:46.323 --> 00:02:50.842
twenty-four Resource Graph queries
and nine Resource Manager GETs,

47
00:02:50.842 --> 00:02:54.232
each with its query or path,
its API version, its role,

48
00:02:54.232 --> 00:02:58.186
which is Reader everywhere, and
the Learn page it comes from.

49
00:02:58.186 --> 00:03:00.310
Fifteen are verified on Learn.

50
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.590
The other eighteen say what's missing,

51
00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.385
and a live run refuses
them unless you allow it.

52
00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:10.624
Two invokers serve the calls: the fixture
invoker reads files and sends nothing;

53
00:03:10.624 --> 00:03:14.910
the live invoker runs a scoped
Resource Graph query or a GET on the

54
00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.910
commercial Resource Manager host, after
checking the CLI's cloud and tenant.

55
00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:23.266
The kit knows one cloud, commercial Azure,

56
00:03:23.266 --> 00:03:27.970
which Microsoft 365 GCC pairs
with, and has no default.

57
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.282
The baseline check reports six rules.

58
00:03:34.282 --> 00:03:37.254
Two come straight from
Learn's hierarchy guidance:

59
00:03:37.254 --> 00:03:40.439
creating management groups
must need authorization,

60
00:03:40.439 --> 00:03:44.544
and new subscriptions must land in a
default group, not the tenant root.

61
00:03:44.544 --> 00:03:46.241
One is the program's design:

62
00:03:46.241 --> 00:03:50.095
the required assignments must
sit at the intermediate root.

63
00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:52.432
One is information, not a finding:

64
00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:56.845
an initiative in DoNotEnforce
is exactly what Step 1 intends.

65
00:03:56.845 --> 00:03:59.907
Then logs: every platform
resource must send

66
00:03:59.907 --> 00:04:02.720
an enabled log to the platform workspace.

67
00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.608
And Defender for Cloud: the
required plans must be on Standard.

68
00:04:06.608 --> 00:04:09.970
The types and plans it checks
live in a configuration

69
00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:13.370
file you change only in
a reviewed pull request.

70
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:18.170
Who's involved?

71
00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:22.315
The platform owner owns the hierarchy,
the logging and the weekly run,

72
00:04:22.315 --> 00:04:26.315
and answers for the findings, with
the security officer reviewing

73
00:04:26.315 --> 00:04:30.170
compliance beside them, as the
guide's Step 1 names the gate owner.

74
00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:35.220
Developers build and review the kit and
keep its call list and rules honest.

75
00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.679
And the reader the kit signs in as needs
only Reader: on the tenant root group

76
00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:44.282
for the hierarchy settings, and on the
intermediate root for everything else.

77
00:04:44.282 --> 00:04:46.460
Nothing in this chapter writes.

78
00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:54.909
Lab 1 needs only PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder, against the

79
00:04:54.909 --> 00:04:58.095
fictional Contoso estate:
an intermediate root,

80
00:04:58.095 --> 00:05:01.122
four subscriptions and
a platform workspace.

81
00:05:01.122 --> 00:05:02.323
No subscription.

82
00:05:02.323 --> 00:05:08.147
Step 1 lists the six calls this chapter
makes, every one Reader, every one verified.

83
00:05:08.147 --> 00:05:13.195
Step 2 runs the baseline check on the
first week's estate: hierarchy settings,

84
00:05:13.195 --> 00:05:18.723
four policy assignments, sixteen platform
resources and four subscriptions read.

85
00:05:18.723 --> 00:05:22.686
Twelve findings and one
information row, from six calls,

86
00:05:22.686 --> 00:05:27.180
every one a read, with a manifest
that lists each file's hash.

87
00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.180
Step 3 reads the findings.

88
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.330
Creating management groups
doesn't need authorization.

89
00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:41.645
Ten resources send no log to the platform
workspace; one of them, the firewall's

90
00:05:41.645 --> 00:05:43.306
public IP, does send logs,

91
00:05:43.306 --> 00:05:47.293
but to an application team's
workspace, which doesn't count.

92
00:05:47.293 --> 00:05:50.318
The online subscription's
Key Vault plan is Free.

93
00:05:50.318 --> 00:05:54.155
And the FedRAMP initiative is
in audit, as Step 1 intends.

94
00:05:54.155 --> 00:05:57.016
Step 4 reads the summary the metrics use:

95
00:05:57.016 --> 00:06:01.005
six of sixteen platform
resources log to the workspace.

96
00:06:01.005 --> 00:06:05.867
Step 5 prints, without sending it, the
exact command a live run would send:

97
00:06:05.867 --> 00:06:09.840
the guide's own query, scoped
to the intermediate root.

98
00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.813
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant with a test

99
00:06:15.813 --> 00:06:19.040
management group and
subscription, never production.

100
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.450
Sign in to commercial Azure,
write the settings file from the

101
00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:26.202
template with every placeholder
filled, and run the same check live.

102
00:06:26.202 --> 00:06:30.927
Every call this check makes is verified
on Learn, so it needs no special flag.

103
00:06:30.927 --> 00:06:35.080
Then compare with the guide's own
Validate commands: the assignment list,

104
00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:38.610
the hierarchy settings read
straight from Resource Manager,

105
00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:42.140
and one flagged resource's
diagnostic settings in the portal.

106
00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:46.691
Nothing changed in Azure, so backing
out means deleting what the lab wrote,

107
00:06:46.691 --> 00:06:49.302
which maps the test
estate, and signing out.

108
00:06:49.302 --> 00:06:51.660
The authors haven't run this lab.

109
00:06:55.410 --> 00:07:00.010
Each output is evidence for something
specific, from the guide's requirements table.

110
00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:04.220
The hierarchy settings, the initiatives
at the intermediate root and their

111
00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:08.698
enforcement mode support baseline
configuration and configuration settings.

112
00:07:08.698 --> 00:07:10.894
Which resources log to the workspace,

113
00:07:10.894 --> 00:07:15.711
and which subscriptions have the required
plans, support continuous monitoring.

114
00:07:15.711 --> 00:07:18.872
And the manifest shows
every call was a read,

115
00:07:18.872 --> 00:07:22.210
as Reader, with every
response kept and hashed.

116
00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:26.298
The kit's output supports those
controls; the assessor decides.

117
00:07:26.298 --> 00:07:30.887
And the key security indicators are
FedRAMP's indicators for cloud providers:

118
00:07:30.887 --> 00:07:35.260
the agency lines its own evidence up
with them, and doesn't report them.

119
00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:41.885
What breaks in production,
and what guards against it?

120
00:07:41.885 --> 00:07:46.256
A live run on the wrong cloud or tenant
reports another estate as this one,

121
00:07:46.256 --> 00:07:50.116
so the kit checks the CLI's cloud
and tenant before its first call,

122
00:07:50.116 --> 00:07:51.573
and has no default cloud.

123
00:07:51.573 --> 00:07:55.449
A query without a scope covers
whatever the account happens to see,

124
00:07:55.449 --> 00:07:58.973
so every query takes the management
group from the settings.

125
00:07:58.973 --> 00:08:02.273
A response's next link
could point at another host,

126
00:08:02.273 --> 00:08:06.459
so any URL off the commercial
Resource Manager host is refused.

127
00:08:06.459 --> 00:08:11.037
An unverified call could misread
a property and report a false gap,

128
00:08:11.037 --> 00:08:15.448
so it's refused in a live run unless
you allow it, and Lab 2 compares.

129
00:08:15.448 --> 00:08:19.340
And the run folder never
goes in a public repository.

130
00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.440
Here's what done looks like.

131
00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:28.872
Every call the code makes is in the call
list, with its role and Learn page,

132
00:08:28.872 --> 00:08:32.477
and the test that compares
declared and made calls passes.

133
00:08:32.477 --> 00:08:36.828
Every rule the code can report is
in the rules file, with its source.

134
00:08:36.828 --> 00:08:41.564
The required plans and platform types
match the agency's decision records.

135
00:08:41.564 --> 00:08:43.535
Lab 2 ran in a test subscription,

136
00:08:43.535 --> 00:08:47.552
and the unverified calls' output
was checked against the portal.

137
00:08:47.552 --> 00:08:51.002
And output folders live
outside any repository.

138
00:08:51.002 --> 00:08:55.630
Next, Chapter 2 builds the public
exposure register on the same spine.
